Give chrome://settings the new tab page's design.

One accent, one outline, one pair of text colours, light and dark - the values
chrome://aerium-newtab already draws with, so the first page of the browser and
its settings agree instead of being two designs in one window.

WHY THIS IS ALMOST ENTIRELY VARIABLES. Settings is hundreds of shadow roots and
a stylesheet in the document cannot reach into any of them. What it can do is
set custom properties, which inherit through shadow boundaries, and every one of
those roots takes its colours from the same small set of tokens. Restating the
tokens therefore restyles the whole page - and a component upstream adds next
milestone arrives already themed, which a pile of per-component rules would not.

The selector is html:root rather than html. cr_shared_vars.css has an
html[webui-refresh-2026] block, and a bare html would lose to it on specificity;
:root brings this to the same weight, and settings_vars.css is imported after
cr_shared_vars, so it wins on order instead.

Both --cr-* and --color-* are restated, and that is not belt and braces. The
controls resolve --color-* first and fall back to the cr palette only when it is
absent, so an accent stated only at the cr level never reaches a toggle, a
checkbox or a button - cr_toggle.css and cr_button.css read
--color-toggle-button-track-on and --color-button-background-prominent before
anything else. The colour pipeline declares those on the document; this sheet is
adopted, and adopted sheets cascade after linked ones, so it lands on top.

CARDS ARE AN OUTLINE, NOT A SHADOW, which is the design's one structural change.
A box-shadow with no blur and one pixel of spread draws exactly a hairline ring,
and settings_section.css already puts --cr-card-shadow on #card, so the whole
change is a token: transparent fill, ring, 18px radius. A border would have meant
editing an element whose padding upstream has already tuned.

The dark ground is #060B16, which is the value aerium-brand-surfaces gives
ui::kColorSysBase, so the page and the window frame around it are the same colour
rather than two darks that nearly match. The light ground is #F2F7FD, the new tab
page's own.

The one rule that is not a variable is the section label in
settings_page/settings_section.css. It is the only piece of type on the page that
names a group rather than a setting, and the design says that with 11px, half a
pixel of tracking, upper case and the accent - the same treatment the new tab
page's settings panel gives its own group headings. It keeps
var(--cr-primary-text-color) as the fallback so the file still reads correctly on
its own.

Not done here: the app menu, the toolbar and the other WebUI pages
(chrome://history, chrome://downloads, chrome://extensions). Those read the same
tokens but from cr_shared_vars.css rather than this file, so they are a second
patch, not a wider selector in this one.

Verified against the real 152.0.7977.82 sources: both files were fetched at that
tag, edited, and the patch generated from the pair, then re-applied to a clean
copy of them - it lands with no fuzz and reproduces both files byte for byte.
Neither file is touched by any other patch in the series, checked by grepping
patches/ for both paths.

--- a/chrome/browser/resources/settings/settings_page/settings_section.css
+++ b/chrome/browser/resources/settings/settings_page/settings_section.css
@@ -19,16 +19,21 @@
   padding-inline-end: var(--cr-section-padding);
 }
 
+/* Aerium: the section label is the one on the new tab page's own settings
+ * panel - small, spaced, upper case, in the accent - rather than a slightly
+ * larger run of body text. It is the only piece of type on this page that
+ * names a group rather than a setting, and this is how the design says that. */
 #header .title {
-  color: var(--cr-primary-text-color);
-  font-size: 108%;
-  font-weight: 400;
-  letter-spacing: .25px;
+  color: var(--aerium-accent, var(--cr-primary-text-color));
+  font-size: 11px;
+  font-weight: 600;
+  letter-spacing: .5px;
   margin-bottom: 12px;
   margin-top: var(--cr-section-vertical-margin);
   outline: none;
   padding-bottom: 4px;
   padding-top: 8px;
+  text-transform: uppercase;
 }
 
 #feedback {
--- a/chrome/browser/resources/settings/settings_vars.css
+++ b/chrome/browser/resources/settings/settings_vars.css
@@ -30,3 +30,100 @@
     --settings-error-color: var(--google-red-300);
   }
 }
+
+/* --- Aerium: the new tab page's palette, applied to Settings.
+ *
+ * These are the same values chrome://aerium-newtab draws with, so the first
+ * page of the browser and its settings agree: one accent, one outline, one
+ * pair of text colours, light and dark.
+ *
+ * Everything here is a variable rather than a rule. Settings is hundreds of
+ * shadow roots and a stylesheet in the document cannot reach into them, but
+ * every one of those roots reads its colours from these tokens, so restating
+ * the tokens restyles the lot. It also means an upstream component added next
+ * milestone arrives already themed.
+ *
+ * html:root, not html, on purpose. cr_shared_vars.css has an
+ * html[webui-refresh-2026] block, and a bare html selector would lose to it on
+ * specificity. :root brings this to the same weight, and this file is imported
+ * after that one, so it wins on order instead.
+ *
+ * The --color-* tokens are here for the same reason the --cr-* ones are: the
+ * controls read --color-* first and fall back to the cr palette only when it
+ * is absent, so an accent stated only at the cr level never reaches a toggle
+ * or a button. They are declared on the document, which is where the colour
+ * pipeline puts them, and this sheet is adopted after that one. */
+html:root {
+  --aerium-bg: #F2F7FD;
+  --aerium-surface: #FFFFFF;
+  --aerium-outline: #D8E6F5;
+  --aerium-outline-dim: #E1ECF9;
+  --aerium-accent: #2C6BAE;
+  --aerium-on-accent: #FFFFFF;
+  --aerium-text: #0B2138;
+  --aerium-label: #4A5D7A;
+
+  --md-background-color: var(--aerium-bg);
+  --color-webui-page-background: var(--aerium-bg);
+
+  /* A hairline ring instead of a drop shadow. A box-shadow with no blur and
+   * one pixel of spread draws exactly the outline the design asks for, and it
+   * arrives through a token every card already reads - the alternative is a
+   * border on an element whose padding upstream has already tuned. */
+  --cr-card-background-color: transparent;
+  --color-webui-card-background: transparent;
+  --cr-card-shadow: 0 0 0 1px var(--aerium-outline);
+  --cr-card-border-radius: 18px;
+
+  --cr-menu-background-color: var(--aerium-surface);
+  --cr-menu-background-focus-color: var(--aerium-outline-dim);
+  --cr-menu-shadow: 0 0 0 1px var(--aerium-outline);
+  --cr-dialog-background-color: var(--aerium-surface);
+  --color-webui-dialog-background: var(--aerium-surface);
+  --cr-dialog-border-radius: 16px;
+
+  --cr-separator-color: var(--aerium-outline-dim);
+  --cr-scrollable-border-color: var(--aerium-outline);
+  --cr-focused-item-color: var(--aerium-outline-dim);
+  --cr-hairline: 1px solid var(--aerium-outline-dim);
+
+  --cr-primary-text-color: var(--aerium-text);
+  --cr-secondary-text-color: var(--aerium-label);
+  --cr-title-text-color: var(--aerium-label);
+  --cr-form-field-label-color: var(--aerium-label);
+  --cr-link-color: var(--aerium-accent);
+  --cr-checked-color: var(--aerium-accent);
+  --cr-focus-outline-color: var(--aerium-accent);
+  --iron-icon-fill-color: var(--aerium-label);
+
+  --color-primary-foreground: var(--aerium-text);
+  --color-secondary-foreground: var(--aerium-label);
+  --color-link-foreground-default: var(--aerium-accent);
+  --color-checkbox-foreground-checked: var(--aerium-accent);
+  --color-toggle-button-track-on: var(--aerium-accent);
+  --color-toggle-button-thumb-on: var(--aerium-on-accent);
+  --color-toggle-button-track-off: transparent;
+  --color-toggle-button-thumb-off: var(--aerium-label);
+  --color-button-border: var(--aerium-outline);
+  --color-button-foreground: var(--aerium-accent);
+  --color-button-background-prominent: var(--aerium-accent);
+  --color-button-foreground-prominent: var(--aerium-on-accent);
+  --color-button-background-tonal: var(--aerium-outline-dim);
+  --color-button-foreground-tonal: var(--aerium-text);
+}
+
+@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
+  html:root {
+    /* The base here is the one aerium-brand-surfaces gives ui::kColorSysBase,
+     * so the page and the window frame around it are the same colour rather
+     * than two darks that nearly match. */
+    --aerium-bg: #060B16;
+    --aerium-surface: #0E1834;
+    --aerium-outline: #22376E;
+    --aerium-outline-dim: #1B2A57;
+    --aerium-accent: #7FC4E4;
+    --aerium-on-accent: #06283D;
+    --aerium-text: #D8E6F5;
+    --aerium-label: #8FA3C4;
+  }
+}
